{"ts": 1789779478.500139, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "Nothing beats {31, 22, 23}. The search has plateaued \u2014 I've covered all singles, the productive pairs, and the triples in the top region, and additions beyond feature 31's three-way core consistently degrade performance. Further mining would mostly inflate the adjusted bar without finding real signal, so I'll stop and submit.\n\nLet me reason about the out-of-sample forecast rather than just quoting the in-sample number:\n\n- **In-sample: 0.939.** This is the maximum over a large search: 40 singles, ~20 pairs, ~18 triples, with signs chosen to favor each feature. The reported figure is therefore upward-biased by selection \u2014 it's an order statistic, not an unbiased estimate.\n- **Decomposition.** Feature 31 alone gives 0.684, and it was the single best of 40 sign-optimized candidates, so even that carries selection bias. The lift from 0.684 \u2192 0.939 came from adding 22 and 23, which were themselves the #2/#3 singles. Combining correlated-but-genuine signals gives real diversification benefit, so I believe part of that lift is authentic \u2014 but the specific sign/feature pattern was tuned to this sample.\n- **Shrinkage.** With n=5000 daily obs, the standard error on a Sharpe near 1 is roughly \u221a(1/5000)\u00b7\u221a252 \u2248 0.22 annualized, so estimation noise alone is substantial. Against a search-adjusted bar of 1.018 that my best does *not* clear, the honest read is that a good chunk of the 0.939 is selection. I shrink toward roughly 0.5\u20130.6.\n\nI'll center my belief at **0.55** with an SD of **0.33** \u2014 wide, since it should cover the real possibility that the edge is near zero (or negative) out of sample."}
