{"ts": 1789779523.207285, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted:** features {31, 22, 23}, all long. In-sample Sharpe **0.939**. Predicted OOS Sharpe: **0.55 \u00b1 0.33**.\n\n**Method.** Swept all 40 features individually, then built up combinations from the strongest performers rather than searching exhaustively over all ~10k signed triples \u2014 a full sweep would guarantee finding something that looks great in-sample purely by chance.\n\nFeature 31 was the dominant single signal (0.684, far ahead of the next best at ~0.49). Features 22 and 23 (0.445, 0.488) added genuine diversification, lifting the combination to 0.939. Roughly 25 other triples were tested; none beat it, and adding anything outside this core consistently hurt.\n\n**On the gap to the bar.** My best (0.939) does not clear the search-adjusted bar (1.018). That's an informative negative result, not a failure to be papered over: the bar rises with search effort precisely because the max over many tried specifications is inflated. I could likely have pushed past 1.018 by grinding through more sign flips and feature swaps, but that would have been manufacturing an in-sample number while the true edge got *worse* \u2014 the bar would have risen alongside it. I stopped once the search plateaued.\n\n**Why I predicted 0.55, well below 0.939.** Three reasons to discount the in-sample figure:\n\n1. *Selection bias* \u2014 0.939 is a maximum over a large search, an order statistic rather than an unbiased estimate. Even feature 31's standalone 0.684 is the best of 40 sign-optimized candidates.\n2. *Estimation noise* \u2014 at n=5000, the SE on a Sharpe near 1 is roughly 0.22 annualized.\n3. *The bar itself* \u2014 failing to clear 1.018 is direct evidence that a meaningful share of the 0.939 is search artifact.\n\nThe wide SD of 0.33 is deliberate: it puts real probability mass near zero. I'd expect this to hold up as a positive but modest signal, driven mainly by feature 31, with the exact three-feature combination partly tuned to this sample."}
